## Really cool improvements (ranked by impact-to-effort)

### 1. ✅ Anthropic prompt caching — DONE (2026-06-22)
The `soul` system prompt and the full tool-schema list are now sent with
`cache_control: {"type": "ephemeral"}` on the system block and the last tool definition
(`gateway/llm/providers/anthropic.py`). Anthropic caching is prefix-based, so marking the last
tool caches the whole tools array. Anthropic-only — OpenAI-compat providers have no equivalent.

### 2. ✅ Parallel tool execution — DONE (2026-06-22)
Tool calls within one turn now run concurrently via `asyncio.gather`
(`gateway/runner/agent_runner.py`) instead of a sequential `for` loop. `gather()` preserves
input order in its results regardless of completion order, so message ordering stays
deterministic even though trace events (`tool_call`/`tool_result`) now stream in actual
completion order rather than block order.

### 3. ✅ Model fallback chains — DONE (2026-06-22)
`agent.yaml` can now declare `model_fallbacks: [...]` (`gateway/models/agent.py`). On a
retryable error (429/5xx/529/timeout/connection error), the runner retries the same model with
exponential backoff (`limits.llm_retry_attempts`/`llm_retry_base_delay_seconds`,
`gateway/models/config.py`), then falls over to the next model in the chain once retries are
exhausted (`gateway/runner/agent_runner.py:_call_llm`). Non-retryable errors (4xx other than 429)
skip the retry and fall over immediately. `ProviderError` now carries an optional `status_code`
(`gateway/llm/providers/base.py`) so the runner can classify errors — populated by all 5
providers. New trace events: `llm_retry`, `model_fallback`. Once a fallback succeeds, later
iterations in the same run try it first rather than flapping back to the original model.

Note: the doc's original reference to `e80ea1b Added fallback` was a red herring — that commit
is the unrelated Qwen `reasoning`-field-empty-content fallback in `ollama.py`, not model
fallback chains. No prior work on this existed.

### 4. ✅ Self-observability (`/metrics` + OTel) — DONE (prior to 2026-06-22)
Implemented via the Prometheus `/metrics` endpoint (`gateway/metrics.py`, runs/tokens/tool
latency histograms/MCP restart counts) and OpenTelemetry spans per run/tool-call
(`gateway/tracing.py`, `agent_runner.py`). See README "Prometheus Metrics" and "observability"
config sections.

### 5. ✅ Per-agent tool allowlists — DONE (2026-06-22)
`AgentConfig.tools` (`gateway/models/agent.py`) now accepts entries that are either a bare server
name (every tool, original behaviour) or a `{server_name: [tool_a, tool_b]}` mapping to scope it
down. `AgentConfig.tool_scopes()` normalizes both forms; `MCPManager.get_tools_for_agent`
(`gateway/mcp/manager.py`) filters on it. Fully backward compatible — existing `tools: [grafana,
atlassian]` lists behave exactly as before.

As a follow-on (same session): added an "MCP Tools" page to the **P-Ork** UI
(`../P-Ork/service/src/ui.py:ui_mcp`, `../P-Ork/service/templates/mcp.html`, nav link in
`base.html`) that calls the gateway's existing `GET /mcp/tools` + `GET /mcp/servers` REST
endpoints (no auth required, already returned name/description/inputSchema per tool and
running/pid/restart_count per server — no gateway changes needed for this). Browseable per
server, with tool schemas expandable as JSON. OpenClaw isn't included — it has no REST tool
introspection endpoint, only the WS RPC agent-listing API.

### 6. ✅ Reuse HTTP clients — DONE (2026-06-22)
`OllamaProvider`, `OllamaCloudProvider`, `OpenRouterProvider`, and `GoogleProvider` now create one
`httpx.AsyncClient` in `__init__` and reuse it for every `complete()` call instead of opening a
fresh client (and TCP+TLS handshake) per request. `BaseProvider.aclose()` (default no-op,
`gateway/llm/providers/base.py`) lets each provider release its client; `AnthropicProvider`
already had a single persistent `AsyncAnthropic` client, so it just gained an `aclose()` too.
`LLMRouter.aclose()` (`gateway/llm/router.py`) closes every provider it has lazily instantiated;
called from the gateway's shutdown sequence in `gateway/main.py` (after `mcp_manager.stop_all()`).

### 7. ✅ Concurrency control + real cancellation — DONE (2026-06-22)
New `limits.max_concurrent_runs` (default `10`, `gateway/models/config.py`) backs a gateway-wide
`asyncio.Semaphore` (`_state["run_semaphore"]`, created in `gateway/main.py` lifespan). Each
`agent` request's run is wrapped in an `asyncio.create_task` and raced against a
`_wait_for_disconnect(ws)` task via `asyncio.wait(..., return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)`
(`gateway/main.py:rpc`) — whichever finishes first decides the outcome:

- Run finishes first → proceed normally, cancel the disconnect-watcher.
- Disconnect detected first (whether the run was still queued on the semaphore or already
  executing) → `run_task.cancel()` and stop, instead of letting the agentic loop burn LLM/tool
  calls for a response nobody will receive. No final frame is sent (the socket is already gone).

`AgentRunner.run()` (`gateway/runner/agent_runner.py`) gained an `except asyncio.CancelledError:`
clause that tags the metrics status `"aborted"` before re-raising — previously a cancelled run
would have fallen through to the `finally` block with whatever `run_status` was last set to
(`"ok"` by default), silently mis-recording cancelled runs as successful.

Verified end-to-end against the real FastAPI app via `starlette.testclient.TestClient` with two
real WebSocket connections and a patched slow `AnthropicProvider.complete`: with
`max_concurrent_runs: 1`, a second concurrent request's LLM call measurably started only after
the first's completed (serialized, not concurrent); and closing a connection mid-run produced an
observed `CancelledError` inside the in-flight fake LLM call rather than letting it run to
completion.

### Smaller but worthwhile
- **Startup config validation**: a model string referencing an unconfigured provider fails at
  request time with a raw `KeyError`, not at boot. Validate agents' models against `providers:`
  on load.
- **Auth is thin**: challenge nonce is generated but never verified, `privateKeyPem` is `""`,
  tokens are plaintext UUIDs in a file, default bind is `0.0.0.0` with no TLS. Fine for a
  trusted LAN; worth a security pass before anything wider.
- **No `/health` endpoint** and **no automated tests** — the SPEC describes manual test
  milestones, but there's no `tests/` dir, which makes the refactors above riskier than needed.

