## Bug fixed: OpenRouter (and Google/Ollama) 200-OK error envelopes crashed the run — FIXED (2026-06-23)

Found while debugging a real failure: the `otel-triage-verified` pipeline's `first-line-support`
step (an OpenRouter free-tier model) crashed with a raw `KeyError: 'choices'` instead of a clean
error. Root cause: OpenRouter (and presumably Google/Ollama, same OpenAI-compat response shape)
can return **HTTP 200 OK with an `{"error": ...}` body** instead of `choices` when the upstream
model provider it's proxying to fails — common on free-tier models under load. `httpx`'s
`raise_for_status()` never catches this since the status is 200, so the old code went straight to
`data["choices"][0]` and blew up with an unclassified `KeyError` that bypassed the retry/fallback
logic entirely (#3) — bypassed because that logic only fires on classified `ProviderError`s.

This bug predates everything in this doc (introduced 2026-05-26, the original LLM-executor
commit) — the retry/fallback work in #3 didn't cause it, but it does mean #3's retry/fallback
logic was silently inert against this specific failure mode until now.

Fix: new `raise_if_error_envelope(data, provider_label)` helper in
`gateway/llm/providers/base.py`, called right after `resp.json()` in `openrouter.py`, `google.py`,
and both providers in `ollama.py`, before any `data["choices"]`/`data["message"]` indexing. Raises
a classified `ProviderError` (status code from `error.get("code")` if present, else `None` —
which the runner already treats as retryable) so this failure mode now flows through the existing
retry + model-fallback machinery instead of crashing the run. Verified: reproduced the exact 200+
error-envelope response via a mocked `httpx` transport, confirmed a clean `ProviderError` is now
raised instead of `KeyError`, confirmed normal success responses are unaffected, and confirmed
the failure now retries and recovers cleanly through `AgentRunner.run()` end-to-end.

